## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` / `Join(timeout)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — equivalent to the old ThreadAbortException block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; we simply continue to loop exit
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, there's nothing else we can do —
        // Thread.Abort doesn't work in .NET 6+. Log a warning instead.
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
        {
            // Log: "Worker thread did not exit within timeout"
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept and respect the token, otherwise cancellation stalls:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely
private WorkItem GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: unblocks when cancelled
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken) throws
// OperationCanceledException when token fires
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `GetNextItem()` won't unblock on cancellation — the thread hangs forever. Every blocking call in the chain must accept the token. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` is CPU-bound with no token checks, it won't stop until it returns. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long processing. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **No fallback for uncooperative code** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, there is no way to forcibly kill a managed thread in .NET 8. If third-party code blocks without token support, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill`. |

---

### If You Truly Need a Hard Kill (Uncooperative Code)

```csharp
// Last resort: run work in a separate process
var process = Process.Start("WorkerApp.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
}
```

This is the only reliable equivalent to the old `Thread.Abort` for code that cannot cooperate.
